WHAT YOU MAY DO
---------------
Use this model in simulation and visualisation. Load it, drive it, modify it,
convert it to MJCF, USD, glTF or anything else, and share your version - with
attribution, and licensed under these same terms.

Converting the model to another format and publishing that conversion is
expressly fine. That is what this is for.


WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO
-------------------
Use it commercially, or redistribute it commercially. Non-commercial sharing is
allowed and encouraged; putting it in a product, a paid service, or a commercial
dataset is not.

If you want to use it commercially, ask. We would rather say yes to a specific
request than publish terms that stop you asking.

    info@norirobotics.com


ONE LICENSE, NOT TWO
--------------------
An earlier draft split this: a permissive license on the description and a
non-commercial one on the meshes. That is a common arrangement and it is what
several robot vendors ship, but it does not fit here - a permissive license
allows commercial redistribution of the part it covers, which is the one thing
these terms are meant to prevent. A single license across the package is also
simply easier to reason about.


PATENTS
-------
No patent rights are granted. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 licenses copyright only, and says
so explicitly (Section 2(b)(2): "Patent and trademark rights are not licensed
under this Public License").

This is deliberate. A permissive software license such as Apache-2.0 carries an
express patent grant, which would have granted rights in inventions embodied in
this robot before any application was filed.


NOT OPEN SOURCE — AND WE SAY SO
-------------------------------
The NonCommercial condition means this does NOT meet the Open Source Initiative's
definition of open source, which forbids restricting fields of use. Please
describe it as "source available", or "free for research and simulation" - not as
open source.


THIRD-PARTY CONTENT
